course: dictionary
lesson: 10435
title: Trouble in context
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Trouble is a word with more than one meaning, so context matters. Look at the words around it to find out which meaning fits. «I had trouble with my homework» means I found it difficult. It does not mean I was told off.
explain: Often trouble means a problem or a worry, as in «The King's troubles are over.» Sometimes it means you might be punished, as in «You'll be in trouble.» Read the whole sentence, then try your meaning in it and see if it still makes sense.
explain: Trouble can also be a verb, which is a doing word. «Sorry to trouble you» means sorry to bother you. When you see trouble with an -s, -ed or -ing ending, like troubles, troubled or troubling, it is probably a verb.
fact: Trouble is a noun, meaning problems, difficulty or a situation where you might be punished.
fact: Trouble can also be a verb, meaning to worry someone or to bother them.
fact: «I had trouble with my homework» means I found it difficult.
fact: «It's no trouble» means it was not a bother at all.
fact: If you are in trouble (lost, hurt or frightened), find a trusted adult or call for help. Telling an adult is not getting someone into trouble if it keeps people safe.
exercise: «I had trouble with my homework.» What does trouble mean here? | Helping someone with their work | Being told off by a teacher | A ship in danger at sea | *Difficulty, a problem with the work
exercise: «You'll be in trouble» means what? | You will be very tired | You will win a prize | *You might be told off or punished | You will get a new pet
exercise: Mum says «It's no trouble» when you thank her for making tea. What does she mean? | She has made a big mess | *It was not a bother to her, and she was happy to do it | The tea is too hot to drink | She is cross with you
test: «Sorry to trouble you.» Here trouble is a verb. What does it mean? | *To bother or worry someone | To make a loud noise | To find a problem in a machine | To be told off
test: A swimmer in trouble waves an arm. Why does she do this? | *To ask for help because she is in danger | To show she is winning the race | To say hello to her friends | To cool down in the water
exam: Which phrase means a situation of danger? | get into trouble | no trouble at all | *a ship in trouble | have trouble with maths
